      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T16:53:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This raises a question. The hibernate wake pin doesn\u0027t need to have an interrupt handler. It just wakes the EC up and done. The wake is like a power on and follows a normal boot process. The interrupt handle won\u0027t be triggered. IMO, the wake pins are likely to bind to a GPIO, instead of an interrupt.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "77292f7c_e1bb1b39",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T22:42:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the problem here is that the previous code was broken anyway, and this code\ndoesn\u0027t do anything to fix that (mainly because I didn\u0027t know the internals of how wake was implemented), and the best I thought I could do is put in a TODO to flag the breakage.\n\nThe code was broken because it was calling gpio_enable_interrupt on a GPIO signal that had no corresponding entry in EC_CROS_GPIO_INTERRUPTS. The effect was that the gpio_enable_interrupt was ignored (this is different behaviour in Zephyr from the old EC-OS). And it\u0027s the same thing with gpio_rtc_ec_wake_odl.\nSo in effect this pin was being completely ignored as a wake signal, so I made it obvious.\nFixing this seemed out of the scope of this change.",
